{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016ah8ZTPDyY5vmGt9ZBrx5R","type":"tool_result","content":"# Multi-Stage Builds: Key Concepts\n\n**Problem Solved:**\n\"Multi-stage builds are useful to anyone who has struggled to optimize Dockerfiles while keeping them easy to read and maintain.\"\n\n**How FROM Works:**\n\"With multi-stage builds, you use multiple FROM statements in your Dockerfile. Each FROM instruction can use a different base, and each of them begins a new stage of the build.\"\n\n**COPY --from Mechanism:**\n\"You can selectively copy artifacts from one stage to another, leaving behind everything you don't want in the final image.\"\n\n**Build Artifacts Excluded:**\n\"None of the build tools required to build the application are included in the resulting image.\"\n\nAdditionally: \"The Go SDK and any intermediate artifacts are left behind, and not saved in the final image.\""}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"89558532-ef37-43d4-ab5c-e8bf2bfb520f","uuid":"48023da3-8187-4e65-8451-1b7989d5a597","timestamp":"2026-08-24T04:59:11.862Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":6858,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Multi-Stage Builds: Key Concepts\n\n**Problem Solved:**\n\"Multi-stage builds are useful to anyone who has struggled to optimize Dockerfiles while keeping them easy to read and maintain.\"\n\n**How FROM Works:**\n\"With multi-stage builds, you use multiple FROM statements in your Dockerfile. Each FROM instruction can use a different base, and each of them begins a new stage of the build.\"\n\n**COPY --from Mechanism:**\n\"You can selectively copy artifacts from one stage to another, leaving behind everything you don't want in the final image.\"\n\n**Build Artifacts Excluded:**\n\"None of the build tools required to build the application are included in the resulting image.\"\n\nAdditionally: \"The Go SDK and any intermediate artifacts are left behind, and not saved in the final image.\"","durationMs":3180,"url":"https://docs.docker.com/build/building/multi-stage/"}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017jagBZP4ZCpWCTri2ZkV7j","type":"tool_result","content":"# Base Image Selection and Optimization Guidance\n\n## Choosing Official and Verified Images\n\n\"Docker Official Images are a curated collection that have clear documentation, promote best practices, and are regularly updated.\"\n\n\"Verified Publisher images are high-quality images published and maintained by the organizations partnering with Docker.\"\n\n## Using Minimal Base Images\n\n\"When building your own image from a Dockerfile, ensure you choose a minimal base image that matches your requirements. A smaller base image not only offers portability and fast downloads, but also shrinks the size of your image and minimizes the number of vulnerabilities introduced through the dependencies.\"\n\nDocker recommends Alpine as an option: \"tightly controlled and small in size (under 6 MB), while still being a full Linux distribution.\"\n\nThe documentation advises: \"A small image with minimal dependencies can considerably lower the attack surface.\"\n\n## Pinning Base Image Versions\n\nRather than using mutable tags, the guidance states: \"To fully secure your supply chain integrity, you can pin the image version to a specific digest.\"\n\nThis approach ensures that \"even if the publisher updates the `3.21` tag, your builds would still use the pinned image version.\"\n\n## Build Cache Optimization\n\n\"Understanding how the build cache works, and how cache invalidation occurs, is critical for ensuring faster builds.\"\n\nRegarding `apt-get` specifically: \"Always combine `RUN apt-get update` with `apt-get install` in the same `RUN` statement\" to avoid caching issues that can result in outdated packages."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"89558532-ef37-43d4-ab5c-e8bf2bfb520f","uuid":"b4a05282-245c-413f-92e6-1e2992dea52d","timestamp":"2026-08-24T04:59:15.695Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":32180,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Base Image Selection and Optimization Guidance\n\n## Choosing Official and Verified Images\n\n\"Docker Official Images are a curated collection that have clear documentation, promote best practices, and are regularly updated.\"\n\n\"Verified Publisher images are high-quality images published and maintained by the organizations partnering with Docker.\"\n\n## Using Minimal Base Images\n\n\"When building your own image from a Dockerfile, ensure you choose a minimal base image that matches your requirements. A smaller base image not only offers portability and fast downloads, but also shrinks the size of your image and minimizes the number of vulnerabilities introduced through the dependencies.\"\n\nDocker recommends Alpine as an option: \"tightly controlled and small in size (under 6 MB), while still being a full Linux distribution.\"\n\nThe documentation advises: \"A small image with minimal dependencies can considerably lower the attack surface.\"\n\n## Pinning Base Image Versions\n\nRather than using mutable tags, the guidance states: \"To fully secure your supply chain integrity, you can pin the image version to a specific digest.\"\n\nThis approach ensures that \"even if the publisher updates the `3.21` tag, your builds would still use the pinned image version.\"\n\n## Build Cache Optimization\n\n\"Understanding how the build cache works, and how cache invalidation occurs, is critical for ensuring faster builds.\"\n\nRegarding `apt-get` specifically: \"Always combine `RUN apt-get update` with `apt-get install` in the same `RUN` statement\" to avoid caching issues that can result in outdated packages.","durationMs":5414,"url":"https://docs.docker.com/build/building/best-practices/"}}
