{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EX8dcvPEGyYfx9pj2B2wQF","type":"tool_result","content":"# CNCF Cloud Native Glossary: Container Image Definition\n\n**What it is:**\n\"A container image is an immutable, static file containing the dependencies for the creation of a container.\"\n\nThe definition specifies that these dependencies include \"a single executable binary file, system libraries, system tools, environment variables, and other required platform settings.\"\n\n**Problem it addresses:**\nThe glossary explains that traditionally, \"application servers are configured per environment, and applications are deployed to them. Any misconfiguration between environments is problematic and often leads to downtime or failed deployments.\"\n\n**How it helps:**\n\"Container images bundle an application with any of its runtime dependencies, such as an application server. This provides consistency across all environments, including a developer's machine.\"\n\nThe entry also notes that \"container images can be used to instantiate as many containers as needed, allowing for greater scalability.\"\n\n**Additional details:**\nContainer images are \"typically stored in container registries, where they can be downloaded and run as an isolated process using a Container Runtime Interface (CRI)\" and \"must follow the standard schema defined by the Open Container Initiative (OCI).\""}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"89558532-ef37-43d4-ab5c-e8bf2bfb520f","uuid":"1eac48b9-ab6f-4a3a-97ad-1862eb7d05ca","timestamp":"2026-08-24T04:59:44.525Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":47041,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# CNCF Cloud Native Glossary: Container Image Definition\n\n**What it is:**\n\"A container image is an immutable, static file containing the dependencies for the creation of a container.\"\n\nThe definition specifies that these dependencies include \"a single executable binary file, system libraries, system tools, environment variables, and other required platform settings.\"\n\n**Problem it addresses:**\nThe glossary explains that traditionally, \"application servers are configured per environment, and applications are deployed to them. Any misconfiguration between environments is problematic and often leads to downtime or failed deployments.\"\n\n**How it helps:**\n\"Container images bundle an application with any of its runtime dependencies, such as an application server. This provides consistency across all environments, including a developer's machine.\"\n\nThe entry also notes that \"container images can be used to instantiate as many containers as needed, allowing for greater scalability.\"\n\n**Additional details:**\nContainer images are \"typically stored in container registries, where they can be downloaded and run as an isolated process using a Container Runtime Interface (CRI)\" and \"must follow the standard schema defined by the Open Container Initiative (OCI).\"","durationMs":3531,"url":"https://glossary.cncf.io/container-image/"}}
