{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SsD2KopWfch1Np2azKJu6F","type":"tool_result","content":"# CNCF Cloud Native Glossary: Containers Entry\n\n**Definition:**\n\"A container is a running process with resource and capability constraints managed by a computer's operating system.\"\n\n**Problem it addresses:**\n\"Before containers were available, separate machines were necessary to run applications. Each machine would require its own operating system, which takes CPU, memory, and disk space, all for an individual application to function.\"\n\n**How it helps:**\n\"Containers share the same operating system and its machine resources, spreading the operating system's resource overhead and creating efficient use of the physical machine.\"\n\nThe glossary also notes a key limitation: \"Since containers share the same operating system, processes can be considered less secure than alternatives.\"\n\n**Note:** The page does not contain any direct sentence comparing containers to virtual machines, though it does discuss how containers differ from the pre-container approach of using separate machines with individual operating systems."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"89558532-ef37-43d4-ab5c-e8bf2bfb520f","uuid":"8a939941-4f7b-4a28-8d1a-6879d4ef1a09","timestamp":"2026-08-24T05:00:04.552Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":49324,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# CNCF Cloud Native Glossary: Containers Entry\n\n**Definition:**\n\"A container is a running process with resource and capability constraints managed by a computer's operating system.\"\n\n**Problem it addresses:**\n\"Before containers were available, separate machines were necessary to run applications. Each machine would require its own operating system, which takes CPU, memory, and disk space, all for an individual application to function.\"\n\n**How it helps:**\n\"Containers share the same operating system and its machine resources, spreading the operating system's resource overhead and creating efficient use of the physical machine.\"\n\nThe glossary also notes a key limitation: \"Since containers share the same operating system, processes can be considered less secure than alternatives.\"\n\n**Note:** The page does not contain any direct sentence comparing containers to virtual machines, though it does discuss how containers differ from the pre-container approach of using separate machines with individual operating systems.","durationMs":3162,"url":"https://glossary.cncf.io/container/"}}
