{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QTeqgFntJ8WvmirfiNmARL","type":"tool_result","content":"I cannot find any sentence in the provided page content that explicitly states that Windows containers share the host kernel or describes kernel-level isolation in those terms.\n\nThe page does contain this relevant statement about container isolation:\n\n\"This document uses the term _Windows containers_ to mean Windows containers with process isolation. Kubernetes does not support running Windows containers with [Hyper-V isolation](https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/virtualization/windowscontainers/manage-containers/hyperv-container).\"\n\nHowever, this sentence specifies what type of isolation Windows containers use (process isolation) rather than making a statement about sharing the host kernel. The page does not contain verbatim language explicitly stating that Windows containers \"share the host kernel\" with the host system."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"89558532-ef37-43d4-ab5c-e8bf2bfb520f","uuid":"0ce5e2ee-1122-49a9-9d7d-32492ec8af42","timestamp":"2026-08-24T05:00:27.600Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":514952,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"I cannot find any sentence in the provided page content that explicitly states that Windows containers share the host kernel or describes kernel-level isolation in those terms.\n\nThe page does contain this relevant statement about container isolation:\n\n\"This document uses the term _Windows containers_ to mean Windows containers with process isolation. Kubernetes does not support running Windows containers with [Hyper-V isolation](https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/virtualization/windowscontainers/manage-containers/hyperv-container).\"\n\nHowever, this sentence specifies what type of isolation Windows containers use (process isolation) rather than making a statement about sharing the host kernel. The page does not contain verbatim language explicitly stating that Windows containers \"share the host kernel\" with the host system.","durationMs":3288,"url":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/windows/intro/"}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GAj2okvCUbekjzHBehtLJ9","type":"tool_result","content":"# Container Runtimes Documentation - Key Excerpts\n\n## Introduction & Requirement\n\n**Exact sentence about installing container runtime:**\n> \"You need to install a [container runtime](/docs/setup/production-environment/container-runtimes \"The container runtime is the software that is responsible for running containers.\") into each node in the cluster so that Pods can run there.\"\n\n**CRI requirement:**\n> \"Kubernetes 1.36 requires that you use a runtime that conforms with the [Container Runtime Interface](/docs/concepts/architecture/cri \"Protocol for communication between the kubelet and the local container runtime.\") (CRI).\"\n\n## Container Runtimes Documented\n\nThe page documents the following container runtimes:\n- [containerd](#containerd)\n- [CRI-O](#cri-o)\n- [Docker Engine](#docker)\n- [Mirantis Container Runtime](#mcr)\n\n## CRI Socket/Endpoint Paths\n\n**For containerd:**\n> \"On Linux the default CRI socket for containerd is `/run/containerd/containerd.sock`. On Windows the default CRI endpoint is `npipe://./pipe/containerd-containerd`.\"\n\n## Cgroup Drivers Statement\n\n> \"Both the [kubelet](/docs/reference/command-line-tools-reference/kubelet \"An agent that runs on each node in the cluster. It makes sure that containers are running in a pod.\") and the underlying container runtime need to interface with control groups to enforce [resource management for pods and containers](/docs/concepts/configuration/manage-resources-containers/) and set resources such as cpu/memory requests and limits. To interface with control groups, the kubelet and the container runtime need to use a _cgroup driver_. It's critical that the kubelet and the container runtime use the same cgroup driver and are configured the same.\""}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"89558532-ef37-43d4-ab5c-e8bf2bfb520f","uuid":"0c90fb0b-6534-4e01-9cc5-c9b133a82c17","timestamp":"2026-08-24T05:00:32.540Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":508506,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Container Runtimes Documentation - Key Excerpts\n\n## Introduction & Requirement\n\n**Exact sentence about installing container runtime:**\n> \"You need to install a [container runtime](/docs/setup/production-environment/container-runtimes \"The container runtime is the software that is responsible for running containers.\") into each node in the cluster so that Pods can run there.\"\n\n**CRI requirement:**\n> \"Kubernetes 1.36 requires that you use a runtime that conforms with the [Container Runtime Interface](/docs/concepts/architecture/cri \"Protocol for communication between the kubelet and the local container runtime.\") (CRI).\"\n\n## Container Runtimes Documented\n\nThe page documents the following container runtimes:\n- [containerd](#containerd)\n- [CRI-O](#cri-o)\n- [Docker Engine](#docker)\n- [Mirantis Container Runtime](#mcr)\n\n## CRI Socket/Endpoint Paths\n\n**For containerd:**\n> \"On Linux the default CRI socket for containerd is `/run/containerd/containerd.sock`. On Windows the default CRI endpoint is `npipe://./pipe/containerd-containerd`.\"\n\n## Cgroup Drivers Statement\n\n> \"Both the [kubelet](/docs/reference/command-line-tools-reference/kubelet \"An agent that runs on each node in the cluster. It makes sure that containers are running in a pod.\") and the underlying container runtime need to interface with control groups to enforce [resource management for pods and containers](/docs/concepts/configuration/manage-resources-containers/) and set resources such as cpu/memory requests and limits. To interface with control groups, the kubelet and the container runtime need to use a _cgroup driver_. It's critical that the kubelet and the container runtime use the same cgroup driver and are configured the same.\"","durationMs":5827,"url":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/setup/production-environment/container-runtimes/"}}
