{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_018zF3rpnAv35BdHpjTmjEqF","type":"tool_result","content":"# Container Image Layers Explained\n\n**Composition and Immutability:**\n\"container images are composed of layers. And each of these layers, once created, are immutable.\"\n\n**Layer Content:**\n\"Each layer in an image contains a set of filesystem changes - additions, deletions, or modifications.\"\n\n**Layer Reuse Benefits:**\n\"This is beneficial because it allows layers to be reused between images.\" This capability means \"builds faster and reduce the amount of storage and bandwidth required to distribute the images.\"\n\n**How Layers Stack:**\n\"When you run a container from an image, a union filesystem is created where layers are stacked on top of each other, creating a new and unified view.\"\n\nAdditionally, \"When the union filesystem is created, in addition to the image layers, a directory is created specifically for the running container. This allows the container to make filesystem changes while allowing the original image layers to remain untouched.\"\n\n**Layer Ordering:**\nThe documentation demonstrates layer ordering through a five-layer example, showing how each successive layer builds upon the previous ones, with later layers (like application code) depending on foundational layers (like the runtime)."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"89558532-ef37-43d4-ab5c-e8bf2bfb520f","uuid":"a3a6f18c-0bd6-497b-891c-ea0dea1b631b","timestamp":"2026-08-24T05:00:51.001Z","tool_use_result":{"bytes":9829,"code":200,"codeText":"OK","result":"# Container Image Layers Explained\n\n**Composition and Immutability:**\n\"container images are composed of layers. And each of these layers, once created, are immutable.\"\n\n**Layer Content:**\n\"Each layer in an image contains a set of filesystem changes - additions, deletions, or modifications.\"\n\n**Layer Reuse Benefits:**\n\"This is beneficial because it allows layers to be reused between images.\" This capability means \"builds faster and reduce the amount of storage and bandwidth required to distribute the images.\"\n\n**How Layers Stack:**\n\"When you run a container from an image, a union filesystem is created where layers are stacked on top of each other, creating a new and unified view.\"\n\nAdditionally, \"When the union filesystem is created, in addition to the image layers, a directory is created specifically for the running container. This allows the container to make filesystem changes while allowing the original image layers to remain untouched.\"\n\n**Layer Ordering:**\nThe documentation demonstrates layer ordering through a five-layer example, showing how each successive layer builds upon the previous ones, with later layers (like application code) depending on foundational layers (like the runtime).","durationMs":3893,"url":"https://docs.docker.com/get-started/docker-concepts/building-images/understanding-image-layers/"}}
